[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK1NR_b_KQkq_-_3_3"]

{ If he copies your Vienna bishop instead of bringing his king knight out, that copying carries a real price. }
3... Bc5 { He mirrors your bishop move, but his king knight is still asleep at home. What has he left unguarded? } 4. Qg4 { Out she comes, staring straight down the open g column at his pawn on g7, and he has no comfortable answer. [%cal Gg4g7] } (4. Qh5 { The sister jump to h5 works just as well, hitting the same weak point. } 4... Qf6 5. Nf3 Nge7 { Two different roads to the same headache for him. Either queen move is fine. }) (4. Nf3 { Quiet, respectable development, with nothing wrong in it. } 4... Nf6 5. O-O O-O { That settles into a normal game, but the queen jump asks him far harder questions right now. }) (4. d3 { Solid and sound, and it keeps every one of your pieces defended. } 4... Nf6 5. Nf3 h6 { A pleasant, steady edge for you either way. The queen sortie is simply the sharper choice. }) (4. Nd5 { The knight jumps at the hole on d5, but nothing of yours is even attacking that square yet. } 4... Nf6 5. c3 O-O { He develops with a free tempo, and the jump has gained you nothing at all. }) 4... Kf8 { His best try: give up castling rather than lose the pawn. Now tuck your queen somewhere safe. } 5. Qf3 { She slides out of harm's way and still aims straight at f7. [%cal Gf3f7] } (5. Qxg7+ { Look again before grabbing: his king walked to f8, and f8 defends g7. } 5... Kxg7 { The king simply recaptures the queen. You just lost the queen for a single pawn. [%cal Rf8g7] }) (5. Qg3 { Just as good, and every bit as pointed at his weak squares. } 5... Nf6 6. Nge2 h5 { Equally pleasant for you. Either queen square keeps her active. }) (5. Qh5 { She stays out on h5, but he can chase her from there too. } 5... Qf6 6. Nf3 g6 { His pawn hits the queen, and you lose the very tempo you had gained. }) (5. Qd1 { All the way back home. Safe, and it throws away everything the raid achieved. } 5... Qh4 6. Qf3 Nf6 { He seizes the initiative and his king is perfectly fine on f8. }) 5... Nf6 { He finally develops. Bring your own knight out behind the queen. } 6. Nge2 { The king knight, leaving f3 free for the queen. Every one of your pieces still holds its post. [%csl Ge2] } (6. d3 { Solid, but it lets his knight leap into d4 completely unopposed. } 6... Nd4 7. Qd1 c6 { His knight attacks your queen from d4 and chases her all the way home to d1. }) (6. Qd1 { A retreat nobody forced. Now he is suddenly the one with the initiative. } 6... Nd4 7. Nf3 c6 { Three queen moves just to land back on d1, and meanwhile his knight claims d4 for free. }) (6. Bb3 { The bishop steps back while your own king still sits in the centre. } 6... Nd4 7. Qd1 d5 { He grabs the centre with tempo, and you stand clearly worse here. }) 6... Nd4 { His knight jumps at your queen. Trade it off before it settles for good. } 7. Nxd4 { Take it off at once — leave that knight alone and it comes to rule the whole board. [%csl Gd4] } (7. Qd3 { Stepping aside instead lets the knight simply stay where it is. } 7... a5 8. O-O c6 { His knight now sits in the centre for the rest of the game, and nothing challenges it. }) (7. Qg3 { Sidestepping looks safe, but count what his knight actually hits from d4 first. } 7... Nxc2+ 8. Kd1 Nxa1 { The knight forks your king and rook, then eats the rook on a1. You are lost, down a rook and a pawn for nothing. [%cal Rd4c2,Rc2a1] }) (7. Qxf6+ { A queen trade offer, except you are giving up the queen for a mere knight. } 7... Qxf6 8. Nd5 Qd8 { A whole queen traded for a knight. The game is effectively over. [%csl Rf6] }) 7... exd4 { He takes back, and his pawn on d4 now presses against your knight. } 8. Nd5 { Forward, never back: the knight jumps into the very hole his own pawn just made. [%csl Gd5] } (8. Ne2 { Retreating is safe, but the knight ends up doing nothing there. } 8... Qe7 9. c3 Qxe4 { He wins the pawn back with the queen and stands comfortably ahead. }) (8. Na4 { The rim again, and now your knight sits a long way from his king. } 8... Bd6 9. c3 Rb8 { Material is level at best, and your knight is stranded out on the edge. }) (8. e5 { A push that simply gives away the very pawn you were defending. } 8... dxc3 9. exf6 cxd2+ { His pawn crashes through with check, and the whole point of your set-up has gone. }) 1-0


