{ The same Vienna copycat start, but this time his own queen comes out to play the hero. }
4... Qf6 { From f6 her queen now stands between your queen and the pawn on g7. Clever, but where can you hit her and gain something real? } 5. Nd5 { The knight jumps in, attacking her on f6 and eyeing c7 at the same time. [%cal Gd5f6,Gd5c7] } (5. Qxg7 { Wait before grabbing: her queen sits right there on f6, looking straight back at you. } 5... Qxg7 { She simply recaptures. You have just handed over your whole queen for a single pawn. [%cal Rf6g7] }) (5. Qg3 { A sensible retreat that keeps the game roughly level. } 5... Nge7 6. Nf3 Nd4 { Nothing wrong here, but you missed a far sharper answer on the board. }) (5. Qf3 { Offering a trade, though her queen was already the badly placed one. } 5... Nd4 6. Qxf6 Nxf6 { Trading queens here hands away your best attacking chance, and he is perfectly comfortable now. }) (5. d4 { Opening lines in the centre while her queen still sits on f6, exposed. } 5... d6 6. Qg3 Nxd4 { His knight grabs the pawn, and you stand worse for having let the moment pass. }) 5... Qxf2+ { She grabs a pawn with check. Let her come — she will not find her way home. } 6. Kd1 { The only legal move on the whole board. Step aside and smile. [%csl Gd1] } 6... Kf8 { He tucks his own king away. Now look hard at that queen: how do you start closing the net? } 7. Nh3 { The knight attacks her on f2 and covers her most natural escape squares, leaving you clearly for choice even though she can still wriggle free with care. [%cal Gh3f2] } (7. Qxg7+ { Look again before grabbing: his king stands right beside that pawn. } 7... Kxg7 { The king simply eats the queen. That trade ends the game in his favour. [%cal Rf8g7] }) (7. Qf3 { Offering the trade lets her escape the whole net for free. } 7... Qxf3+ 8. Nxf3 Bd6 { Queens leave the board, and the trap you spent three moves building has vanished with them. }) (7. Qe2 { The very same problem: you invite her to trade herself off safely. } 7... Qxe2+ 8. Nxe2 Bd6 { Material stays level, the queen walked out through the front door, and he is the one better placed now. }) (7. Nxc7 { A fork on his rook, but you let the trapped queen go free just to grab it. } 7... d5 8. Qf3 dxc4 { His pawn scoops up your bishop on c4. Your knight does attack the rook on a8, but right now you are the one down material. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the greedy knight"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/xRMDLtKP"]
[Role "exercise"]
[FEN "r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/2b1p3/2B1P1Q1/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1B1K1NR b KQkq - 5 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/2b1p3/2B1P1Q1/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1B1K1NR_b_KQkq_-_5_4"]

{ The same Vienna copycat idea, but this time a knight leaps forward instead of guarding home. }
4... Nd4 { The knight jumps into the centre but does nothing to protect the pawn on g7, still hanging to your queen. What is on offer? } 5. Qxg7 { The pawn falls for free, and now his corner rook is suddenly very nervous. [%cal Gg7h8] } (5. Bxf7+ { A check, but you are giving a whole bishop for one pawn. } 5... Kxf7 { He simply recaptures and walks his king home. The truly free pawn was sitting on g7 instead. [%cal Re8f7] }) (5. Bb3 { Saving the bishop from the knight works, but it is far too slow here. } 5... Nxb3 6. axb3 Nf6 { He trades the knight off anyway, and the free pawn on g7 is gone for good. }) (5. Nd5 { The other knight jump, except now his knight is the one causing you trouble. } 5... g6 6. Qd1 c6 { Your queen retreats with little to show for the whole detour. }) (5. Kd1 { Sidestepping a fork that was never actually there yet. } 5... Kf8 6. Qh5 Qe8 { A wasted move, and now his king sits right beside the pawn on g7, keeping it safe. }) 5... Qf6 { His best try: offer the queen trade to save the rook. } 6. Qxf6 { Take it, and stay a clean pawn up. Anything greedier than this goes wrong. [%csl Gf6] } (6. Qxh8 { The rook looks free, but count her escape squares in that corner first. } 6... Qxh8 { She is trapped and simply eaten. You gave a whole queen for a rook. [%cal Rf6h8] }) (6. Qg3 { Backing the queen off and leaving the rook alone entirely. } 6... Ne7 7. Nf3 Rg8 { He saves everything and stands slightly better, even a pawn down, with his knights so active. }) (6. Bxf7+ { A second bite at f7, and it costs you a bishop again. } 6... Qxf7 7. Qxe5+ Ne6 { Two pawns for a bishop is a poor trade, and your own king still sits undeveloped in the centre. }) (6. Qxf7+ { The other capture with check, except it is guarded twice over. } 6... Qxf7 7. Bxf7+ Kxf7 { Queen traded for queen, then the bishop lost to his recapturing king — you end up the one slightly behind on material. }) 6... Nxf6 { He recaptures. Now finish your own development. } 7. Bd3 { A clean pawn up for nothing given back, and the bishop also defends c2. [%csl Gc2] } (7. Nf3 { Natural enough, but look hard at what his knight on d4 is really aiming at. } 7... Nxc2+ 8. Kd1 Nxa1 { The knight forks your king and rook, then eats the rook on a1. The exchange, and more, is gone. [%cal Rd4c2,Rc2a1] }) (7. Bb3 { Also playable, though the pawn on c2 is still sitting there undefended. } 7... Rg8 8. Kf1 c6 { Material is level-ish, and your own king has lost the right to castle. }) (7. Kd1 { Defending c2 with the king itself works, but it is a clumsy way to do it. } 7... b5 8. Bxb5 Nxb5 { His knight wins back the bishop, and the extra pawn you were up disappears with it. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the gambit accepted"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
