{ His other main defence brings the king knight out first. This is where the real Vienna gambit lives. }
2... Nf6 { His knight attacks your pawn on e4 and defends e5 at the same time. Throw the f pawn at him anyway. [%cal Yf2f4] } 3. f4 { The Vienna gambit: give up the f pawn to rip the position open before he is ready for it. Sound or not, across the board it plays like a monster. [%cal Gf4e5] } (3. Nf3 { The calm road, and a very solid choice in its own right. } 3... Nc6 4. d4 Bb4 { Solid, sound chess for both sides — just not the reason we picked the Vienna today. }) (3. Bc4 { Developing the bishop first, heading straight for f7, is perfectly good too. } 3... Bc5 4. Nge2 O-O { A quiet game either way. The gambit is simply the sharper weapon we want. }) (3. g3 { The quiet Vienna, with a fianchetto still to come. } 3... Bc5 4. Bg2 O-O { Playable and pleasant, but today we came for the fireworks instead. }) (3. d4 { Straight down the middle, but he simply captures it. } 3... exd4 4. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight attacks your queen on d4, and the tempo you spent is handed straight back. }) 3... exf4 { He takes the bait. Now hit the knight before it can settle anywhere. [%cal Ye4e5] } 4. e5 { The whole point of the gambit. Every square his knight can run to is bad or useless. [%cal Ge5f6] } (4. Bc4 { Developing instead of pushing lets him consolidate at once. } 4... c6 5. d4 d5 { He keeps the pawn and builds a wall behind it. Your gambit has bought nothing at all. }) (4. Nf3 { Also natural, and also a move too slow for this position. } 4... d5 5. exd5 Nxd5 { He keeps the extra pawn and still gets a perfectly fine game. }) (4. d4 { A big centre, but you left his knight completely unbothered. } 4... Bb4 5. Bd3 d5 { The pin arrives, and the chance to play e5 yourself has gone for good. }) 4... Ng8 { All the way home. He owns one cold, extra pawn; you own the whole board. [%csl Rg8][%cal Yg1f3] } 5. Nf3 { Develop with a free tempo while his pieces still sleep at home. [%csl Gf3] } (5. Bc4 { The bishop belongs on this square too, but the knight gets there faster. } 5... d6 6. Qe2 dxe5 { He hands the pawn back and untangles. Less than you had before. }) (5. Qe2 { The queen props up the pawn, playable, though she blocks in your own bishop. } 5... Nc6 6. Nf3 g5 { He holds onto the extra pawn, and you are the one pushing uphill now. }) (5. Qf3 { Also playable, and again the queen takes the square your knight wanted. } 5... Nc6 6. Qxf4 d6 { Material comes back level. Developing the knight to f3 first keeps far more pressure on the board. }) 5... d6 { He strikes back at your proud pawn on e5. Answer by building the full centre. [%cal Yd2d4] } 6. d4 { Two big pawns and two knights out, while his pieces are all still at home. [%csl Ge5,Gd4] } (6. Qe2 { Also good, holding the pawn one move longer. } 6... dxe5 7. d4 Be7 { Playable, and the queen can even scoop e5 back later — building with the pawn to d4 simply does the same job with more menace. }) (6. exd6 { Releasing the tension yourself only frees up his game. } 6... Bxd6 7. d4 Ne7 { He develops with a free tempo, and the space you had has gone back to him. }) (6. Bc4 { The bishop again, though the centre was really the bigger prize here. } 6... Nc6 7. Qe2 dxe5 { He unwinds fully, and your grip on the position has loosened to dead level. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the gambit main line"]
[Result "1-0"]
