{ The gambit sits on the board, and now he must find the sharpest answer. So must you. }
3... d5 { His strongest answer: he hits back in the centre at once. Two of your pawns can capture. Which one? } 4. fxe5 { The f pawn captures toward the centre, kicking away the knight's support on e5. [%csl Ge5] } (4. exd5 { The other capture looks natural, and it is the wrong one here. } 4... Nxd5 5. fxe5 Nxc3 6. bxc3 Qh4+ { Her check lands, and defending it costs you the right to castle. }) (4. Nf3 { Developing while leaving both central pawns hanging is too slow. } 4... exf4 5. exd5 Nxd5 { He ends up a clean pawn up with a perfectly free game. }) (4. d3 { Propping the e pawn, but the real fight is in the middle of the board. } 4... d4 5. Nce2 exf4 { His pawn claims the square your knight wanted, and you stand worse. }) (4. d4 { A big centre, but you still owed the position a capture first. } 4... exd4 5. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight attacks your queen on d4, and you stand simply worse. }) 4... Nxe4 { He grabs the pawn straight back. Now hit the knight and eye f7 at the same time. } 5. Qf3 { The queen steps out to f3, attacking the knight on e4 and squinting straight at f7. [%cal Gf3f7,Gf3e4] } (5. Nf3 { The classical road, and every bit as good. } 5... Bc5 6. d4 Bb4 { Also perfectly fine. The queen move is simply the sharper choice today. }) (5. Nxe4 { Trading off your best knight for his own. } 5... dxe4 6. d4 exd3 { That knight on e4 was worth keeping him occupied with instead of trading. }) (5. d3 { Kicking the knight with a pawn, but look at your own pawns afterward. } 5... Nxc3 6. bxc3 d4 { Your pawns end up wrecked before a single piece has even come out. }) 5... Nc6 { He develops and defends the knight. Pile on anyway: it still hangs. } 6. Bb5 { The bishop pins the knight on c6 against his king, while your queen keeps hitting the other one on e4. [%cal Gb5c6,Gf3e4] } (6. Nxe4 { Taking the knight now only lets him off the hook. } 6... Nd4 7. Qc3 dxe4 { His knight lands in the middle with tempo, and you stand worse. }) (6. Qf4 { Defending nothing important and stepping straight into his pawns. } 6... g5 7. Qf3 Nb4 { His pawn kicks the queen and his knight jumps in with a fork on c2 coming. You are in real trouble here. }) (6. Qe2 { Defending the pin square, but all the pressure has drained away. } 6... Bf5 7. Nf3 Bc5 { He has developed every piece while you have developed none. }) 6... Nxc3 { He trades on c3. Recapture toward the centre. } 7. dxc3 { The d pawn recaptures, clearing the diagonal for your bishop on c1 while the queen keeps pressing on f7. [%csl Gc3] } (7. bxc3 { The other pawn recaptures, but it opens nothing useful at all. } 7... Qh4+ 8. Qf2 Qxf2+ { She captures your queen with check — recapture with the king and the queens are off the board, dead level. }) (7. Qxc3 { The queen recaptures, and now she becomes the one getting chased. } 7... Qh4+ 8. Qg3 Qxg3+ { She takes your queen with check again — recapture and the queens leave the board while your king still sits in the centre. }) (7. Bxc6+ { A capture first, though it only helps repair his pawns. } 7... bxc6 8. dxc3 Qh4+ { You have strengthened his central pawns for absolutely nothing. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the gambit declined"]
[Result "1-0"]
