{ Some players simply refuse the gambit outright. That caution hands you a pleasant game for free. }
3... d6 { He props e5 and declines the pawn. Do not grab it back yourself — what does this position actually want? } 4. Nf3 { Develop instead. The tension on e5 is yours to resolve whenever it suits you. [%csl Gf3] } (4. fxe5 { Capturing at once throws away the very tension that was working in your favour. } 4... dxe5 5. Bb5+ c6 { You released the tension for him, his pieces come alive with the check, and you stand worse. }) (4. Bb5+ { A check that only helps him improve his own pawn structure. } 4... c6 5. Be2 Nbd7 { The bishop has to retreat anyway, and he sits perfectly comfortable. }) (4. d4 { A big centre, but his pawn simply takes it and your queen comes out early. } 4... exd4 5. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight gains a tempo on your queen. Playable, but a little loose. }) (4. Be2 { Solid, quiet, and a shade slow for this position. } 4... d5 5. fxe5 Nxe4 { He strikes back in the centre before your own pieces are ready for it. }) 4... Be7 { He develops quietly. Finish bringing your own pieces out. } 5. Be2 { Modest and correct: castle quickly, then decide later what the f pawn should do. [%csl Ge2] } (5. d4 { Also good, and just as direct a way to play it. } 5... exd4 6. Qxd4 Nc6 { A fine game either way. Castling first simply keeps things calmer. }) (5. Bc4 { The usual Vienna square, except here it runs straight into a fork trick. } 5... Nxe4 6. Nxe4 d5 { His pawn forks both your bishop and your knight at once; retreat one, recapture with the other, and the material settles back level. }) (5. fxe5 { Releasing the tension again, and again a move too early. } 5... dxe5 6. Bc4 O-O { Level material, and you have handed back the free hand you had. }) 5... O-O { He tucks his king away safely. Do the very same. } 6. O-O { Castled, extra space, and the f pawn ready to open the column the moment you choose. [%cal Gf1f8] } (6. d4 { Grabbing the centre while your own king still sits in the middle. } 6... exd4 7. Nxd4 c5 { His pawn kicks the knight, and your own king is the one still exposed. }) (6. fxe5 { The same capture again, and it still helps him more than you. } 6... dxe5 7. O-O Nc6 { Level material. The half-open f column was worth more than that trade ever was. }) (6. d3 { Very solid, but you had a completely free move to spend better. } 6... exf4 7. Bxf4 c5 { Material stays equal, and he got to choose the moment the tension broke, not you. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: he ignores the gambit"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/q2gn8vjV"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/5n2/4p3/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_0_3"]
[Role "exercise"]

{He ignores your gambit pawn on f4 and brings out a second knight instead. That lets you open the centre with a capture that also wins a tempo.}
3... Nc6 {His knight develops to c6, leaving the pawn on f4 completely alone. Capture on e5 now and make him spend a move winning it back.} 4. fxe5 {The capture is strong now: his knight on c6 has to leave its post to recover the pawn, so you come out a tempo ahead. [%csl Ge5]} (4. Nf3 {Quiet development lets him take back on f4 at his leisure, so the pawn you offered never really costs him anything.} 4... exf4 5. Bc4 Bb4 {He recaptures on f4 whenever it suits him, and you have nothing to show for the pawn you gave up.}) (4. Bb5 {The pin looks useful, but the pawn on e5 was there to be won first.} 4... exf4 5. Nf3 d5 {He grabs the pawn back and strikes in the centre with his own pawn to d5. The position is level at best.}) (4. d3 {Propping the pawn with a pawn move wastes the moment — the pawn on e5 was yours for the taking.} 4... Bb4 5. fxe5 Nxe5 { He settles in comfortably: by the time you take on e5 his knight is ready to take straight back. }) (4. Nd5 {The knight jumps to d5, defended by the pawn on e4, but it also leaves that same pawn without any other guard.} 4... Nxe4 5. Nf3 Nf6 {He grabs the loose pawn on e4 instead, and you are left both a pawn down and behind in development.}) 4... Nxe5 {His knight recaptures and sits in the middle of the board on e5. Push the pawn to d4 and kick it straight back.} 5. d4 {The pawn on d4 attacks the knight directly, and there is no square left where it stands comfortably. [%cal Gd4e5]} (5. Nf3 {This offers a trade rather than a second tempo — his knight is only too happy to come off on f3.} 5... Nxf3+ 6. Qxf3 d5 {He trades gladly and the position levels out completely. The direct pawn push kept far more pressure on.}) (5. Nge2 {Quiet development leaves his knight sitting comfortably in the centre on e5 for now.} 5... c5 6. d4 cxd4 {He gets time to prop the knight up with his own pawn to c5, and half of your advantage has gone with it.}) (5. Be2 {A modest developing move, but it hands him a free move to strike back in the centre himself.} 5... d5 6. d4 Ng6 {He gets his own pawn to d5 for free, and the position is close to level.}) (5. Bb5 {It looks like a pin toward your king, but his own pawn on d7 blocks the diagonal completely — the bishop is doing nothing at all.} 5... Ng6 6. d4 Bb4 {He gets his knight to safety and his bishop to an active square, and you have spent a tempo for nothing.}) 5... Ng6 {The knight retreats to the rim on g6. Push the pawn to e5 and kick the other knight, still sitting on f6, straight home.} 6. e5 {The pawn advances again, and this time it is his king's knight that has nowhere good to run. [%cal Ge5f6]} (6. Nf3 {Solid development, but it skips the second free kick that the pawn push to e5 was offering.} 6... d5 7. e5 Ne4 {He gets his knight to the strong square on e4 a move earlier than he should, because you let him strike in the centre first.}) (6. Bd3 {The bishop eyes the knight on g6, a fair idea, just slower than simply pushing the pawn.} 6... d5 7. e5 Ne4 {His knight settles on the strong e4 square all the same — exactly what the immediate pawn push to e5 was meant to prevent.}) (6. Qd3 {The queen comes out before your e pawn has finished chasing his last knight home.} 6... Bb4 7. Ne2 O-O {He pins the knight on c3 and then castles into safety. Your edge has shrunk to almost nothing.}) (6. Bc4 {The bishop heads for its favourite diagonal, but it is one move early — the pawn push to e5 was the more urgent job.} 6... c6 7. e5 d5 {You still get the pawn to e5 in, but his pawns on c6 and d5 close the centre and your attack slows right down.}) 6... Ng8 {Both of his knights have been shoved backwards, one to g6 and the other all the way home to g8. Finish developing while he unwinds.} 7. Nf3 {Two big pawns stand in the centre, on d4 and e5, and both of his knights are still tangled up behind them. That lead is the whole reward for ignoring his gambit pawn. [%csl Ge5,Gd4]} (7. h4 {The rook pawn storms forward. It is a sharp try, just a wilder road than simply finishing development first.} 7... d5 8. h5 N6e7 {The knight retreats again and the position stays just as pleasant for you — developing first would have been the calmer road to the same place.}) (7. Bd3 {A fine square for the bishop, pointing straight at h7.} 7... d6 8. Qe2 dxe5 {The position stays comfortable either way — bringing the knight to f3 first simply keeps your initiative rolling a touch faster.}) (7. Qe2 {The queen adds support to the centre pawns. Nothing wrong with it, just less urgent than bringing the last knight out.} 7... Bb4 8. Bd2 Bxc3 { The trade is close to even, and it shows that the knight belonged on f3 all along. }) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the early bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/mk3IpsGO"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_2"]
[Role "exercise"]

{He brings the bishop out before either knight, skipping the Vienna's usual move order entirely. It looks active, but it leaves the centre unsettled.}
2... Bc5 {The bishop comes out early, before either knight has moved. What has he left undone that you can exploit with a natural developing move?} 3. Nf3 {Simple development, and it also prepares the strike in the centre with the pawn to d4 next move. [%csl Gf3]} (3. f4 {The gambit again, but this time his bishop on c5 already guards against the coming storm.} 3... d6 4. Nf3 Nc6 {He props the centre with the pawn to d6 and brings the knight to c6. Every edge from the gambit is gone, and the position is level.}) (3. Qh5 {The early queen raid, aiming at the pawn on e5 and the square f7 in one move.} 3... d6 4. Bc4 Qe7 {Real threats on the board, but he holds everything together with the queen to e7, and yours gets chased away for the trouble.}) (3. Bc4 {Our usual square for the bishop. Perfectly playable, just a shade slower to reach the position we actually want.} 3... Nf6 4. Nf3 d6 {Fine chess, and it often transposes back to the same position anyway — developing the knight first simply gets there in fewer moves.}) (3. Na4 {A knight out to the rim, hunting the bishop all the way from a4.} 3... Bd6 4. Nf3 Nc6 {He keeps the bishop safely on c5, and your knight has spent two whole moves getting nowhere.}) (3. g3 {The quiet fianchetto, but his bishop already sits on c5, eyeing the very centre you are trying to build.} 3... Nf6 4. Bg2 Nc6 {He finishes developing in comfort, and the edge you had from his early bishop has disappeared.}) 3... d6 {He props up the pawn on e5 with the pawn to d6. Now break open the centre with your own pawn to d4.} 4. d4 {There it is — his bishop on c5 must now watch the centre fall, or trade itself off before it does. [%cal Gd4e5]} (4. Na4 {Chasing the bishop with the knight before you break in the centre is just as good an order.} 4... Nf6 5. Nxc5 dxc5 {Equally pleasant. The direct break with the pawn to d4 is simply the more forceful way to reach it.}) (4. Be2 {Quiet development at the exact moment the centre was calling for the pawn break instead.} 4... Nc6 5. Na4 Nge7 {Playable, but the strike in the centre was there for the taking, and this move lets it wait.}) (4. d3 {The little pawn push instead of the big one — d4 hits the bishop on c5 and the pawn on e5 together; d3 hits nothing.} 4... Nc6 5. Na4 Bb6 {Solid and rather dull. His bishop keeps its diagonal, and nothing about the position has really changed.}) 4... exd4 {He captures on d4. Recapture with the knight rather than the pawn, so the piece itself lands in the centre.} 5. Nxd4 {The knight lands in the middle of the board on d4, eyeing both wings, and your pieces have open lines wherever they go. [%csl Gd4]} (5. b4 {The pawn lunges forward to win the bishop, but it leaves the knight on c3 hanging in the middle of the board.} 5... dxc3 6. bxc5 Nf6 {His pawn captures the knight on c3 and yours captures the bishop on c5 right back — material stays level, but pieces are flying everywhere and the position is messy for both sides.}) (5. Ne2 {Retreating the knight instead of capturing. On e2 it has no real future.} 5... Nc6 6. Nexd4 Nxd4 {You take a whole move longer to reach the centre and trade the knights off anyway — nothing at all gained by the detour.}) (5. Nb5 {The knight goes hunting on c7 instead of simply taking the centre for itself.} 5... Nc6 6. Be2 Nf6 {He guards everything comfortably, and your knight ends up stranded on b5 for nothing.}) 5... Nf6 {He develops his last knight. Put the bishop on a square where it does real work.} 6. Bf4 {A pleasant edge: the better centre, the easier position to play, and nothing at all to worry about. [%csl Gd4,Gf4]} (6. Nb3 {Nudging the bishop away first is also good, and arguably the more precise move order.} 6... Bb6 7. Bg5 h6 {Just as pleasant either way — both bishop squares serve the position equally well.}) (6. Bg5 {The pin on the knight is equally good, and a shade more aggressive than the quiet retreat to f4.} 6... O-O 7. Nb3 Bb4 {Also comfortable. Both bishops find useful work from here.}) (6. Bc4 {The bishop returns to its old favourite square, but the pawn it once aimed at on f7 is guarded now.} 6... O-O 7. O-O Nbd7 {Level chess, though the bishop is doing less work on c4 than it would on f4 or g5.}) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the timid d6"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/yXmLc1zx/dLwM07OS"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_2"]
[Role "exercise"]

{Against the Vienna, when he refuses to fight for the centre at all, take every square of it and be glad to.}
2... d6 {A timid little move. It only guards the pawn on e5 and does nothing else at all. Seize the whole centre while you can.} 3. d4 {Straight ahead — he must now either give up the centre entirely or defend it a pawn short. [%csl Gd4]} (3. f4 {The gambit again, but here his pawn on d6 is already propping up the pawn on e5, so the sacrifice buys much less than usual.} 3... exf4 4. Qf3 Nc6 {He takes the pawn on f4 back and develops the knight to c6. You have handed him the whole centre for nothing.}) (3. Nf3 {Solid development. There is nothing wrong with it, it is only that the whole centre was there for the pawn to take.} 3... c5 4. Bc4 Be7 {A perfectly normal game. You turned down the free central space that d4 would have grabbed outright.}) (3. Bc4 {The bishop comes out first. Also playable, just a little less to the point than striking in the centre.} 3... Be7 4. Nf3 c5 {Roughly equal chess. The pawn to d4 asks a far harder question of his position than this does.}) (3. Nge2 {A quiet route for the knight, while the centre was still begging to be taken.} 3... Nf6 4. d4 c6 {You get the pawn to d4 in anyway, just one move later than you needed to.}) (3. g3 {The fianchetto. Solid enough, but it turns down the whole centre that was on offer.} 3... Nf6 4. Bg2 g6 {He gets his pieces out for free while you fianchetto, and the moment to grab the centre has passed.}) 3... Be7 {He tucks the bishop in quietly. Keep building your own centre with a natural developing move.} 4. Nf3 {The knight props up the pawn on d4 and eyes the centre at the same time. Every piece is doing its job. [%csl Gf3]} (4. dxe5 {Trading in the centre only helps him breathe more easily.} 4... dxe5 5. Qxd8+ Bxd8 {The queens come off and the position dries right up. You have gained nothing at all from the trade.}) (4. Be3 {Also good — the bishop backs up the pawn on d4 as a second guard, instead of the knight.} 4... Nf6 5. h3 c6 {Just as pleasant. Either move keeps the same grip on the centre.}) (4. d5 {Locking the centre with a pawn push throws away the freer, more active game you already had.} 4... Nd7 5. a4 Ngf6 {The position closes up, and his cramped pieces suddenly find the squares they needed.}) 4... Nd7 {His pieces are tripping over each other trying to get out. Bring your own bishop into the game.} 5. Bc4 {Every piece of yours sits on a good square, while he is still trying to untangle pieces across half the board. [%csl Gc4,Gd4]} (5. Be3 {Also fine, and just as strong — the bishop can stand guard over that pawn just as well as the knight.} 5... Ngf6 6. h3 c6 {Equally comfortable. Both bishop squares give the piece useful work.}) (5. h3 {A useful little move, if a slow one — nothing on the board was actually threatening you yet.} 5... c6 6. g4 h6 {Also playable, and a rather more committal way to spend the move.}) (5. a4 {Space on the queenside, when the centre is still where the real story is being written.} 5... Ngf6 6. Bc4 O-O {You bring the bishop out anyway, just one move later than in the main line.}) 1-0


[Event "Vienna: the fake pin"]
[Result "1-0"]
