[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_2"]
[Role "exercise"]

{When his bishop aims at your knight and looks like a pin, check what actually happens if the bishop takes — it may not be a real pin at all.}
2... Bb4 {The bishop appears to pin the knight on c3 to your king. But your pawn on d2 is ready to recapture there, so the pin has no bite. [%csl Gd2]} 3. Nd5 {Since the pin does nothing, the knight simply jumps forward, attacking the bishop on b4 and the pawn on c7 at the same time. [%cal Gd5b4,Gd5c7]} (3. d4 {Striking in the centre with the pawn is also strong, and every bit as playable as the knight jump.} 3... d6 4. dxe5 Bxc3+ {His bishop grabs the knight with check on c3. Compared to this, the immediate jump to d5 forces a much harder decision on him straight away.}) (3. a3 {Kicking the bishop with the pawn is playable, but he simply trades it for the knight at once.} 3... Bxc3 4. dxc3 d6 {Your pawns double up on the c column, and his bishop is gone for a knight — a fair trade, not a loss.}) (3. Nf3 {Quiet development, ignoring the bishop's threat completely.} 3... Bxc3 4. dxc3 d6 {He trades on c3 and doubles your pawns for free, since nothing made him think twice about it.}) (3. Qh5 {The early queen raid, aiming at the pawns on e5 and f7 together.} 3... Bxc3 4. bxc3 Qe7 {He holds everything together, and your pawns end up doubled on the c column — though the open lines and the bishop pair make up for it comfortably.}) 3... Bc5 {The bishop runs to a fresh diagonal instead of trading. Open the position while his pieces are still behind.} 4. d4 {Strike in the centre. Whichever way he recaptures, his pieces end up loose in your half of the board. [%cal Gd4e5,Gd4c5]} (4. Nxc7+ { A fork on the king and the rook on a8 — but look hard at c7 before you jump in: something of his is watching that square. [%csl Rc7] } 4... Qxc7 {His queen was watching c7 the whole time and simply recaptures. You have given up a knight for one pawn. [%cal Rd8c7]}) (4. b4 {Kicking the bishop with the wing pawn is just as good a way to open the position.} 4... Bd6 5. Nf3 c6 {Equally pleasant. Striking in the centre with the pawn to d4 is simply the more natural way to do it.}) (4. c3 {Building slowly with the pawn to c3 is also fine, if a touch quieter than the direct strike.} 4... Ne7 5. d4 Nxd5 {You still get the pawn to d4 in, and he trades the knight off along the way.}) (4. Qg4 {The queen comes out and eyes g7, while the knight is still sitting on d5 waiting for a decision.} 4... Bf8 5. Qg3 d6 {He defends everything that matters, and your pieces end up tangled instead of his.}) 4... Bxd4 {He grabs the pawn with the bishop, and that bishop now stands completely alone in your half of the board.} 5. Nf3 {Chase the bishop with the knight. He now has to spend a move saving it while you simply keep developing. [%csl Rd4]} (5. c3 {Kicking the bishop with the pawn works, but it lets him keep the extra pawn he grabbed on d4.} 5... Bc5 6. Qh5 d6 {He keeps the extra pawn and the game is level — chasing the bishop with the knight first was the faster way to punish it.}) (5. Qg4 {The queen comes out again, and the bishop on d4 is still not actually under attack.} 5... Kf8 6. Nf3 d6 {He gives up the right to castle to save the bishop, and still keeps the extra pawn. Not enough compensation for you.}) (5. Ne2 {The other knight route attacks the bishop, but it blocks your own knight from developing to that very square.} 5... Bc5 6. Nec3 d6 {Your own pieces trip over each other trying to get out, and he is perfectly fine.}) (5. f4 {The gambit pawn push, with his bishop still sitting deep in your position on d4.} 5... c6 6. Ne3 Nf6 {He kicks the knight away and keeps everything he has grabbed. You are worse off here.}) 1-0
